You will know the script has found a potential vulnerability through two clear signals: a color-coded alert in your terminal and an automated log entry in your output file.
The script does not verify the bug for you, but it highlights the exact location of a high-risk lead. Here is exactly what to look for:
## 1. The Terminal Alert (Real-Time Visual Clue)
While the script runs, it completely ignores normal comments and only prints text when it matches your high-risk keywords. If it finds a lead, the text will flash bright red with a critical flag. [1] 

* What it looks like on your screen:

[*] Thread started: Scanning target -> https://target.com
[!] CRITICAL HTML COMMENT on https://target.com (Keywords: ['admin', 'todo']): <!-- <a href="/admin-beta-test">TODO: remove entry before launch</a> -->
[!] CRITICAL JS COMMENT in External JS File on https://target.com (Keywords: ['key', 'secret']): const AWS_SECRET = "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
[+] Finished https://target.com - Found 2 high-risk comments logged!


## 2. The Output Log File (Your Hit List)
Once the scan finishes, you do not need to scroll through your terminal history. Open the newly created high_risk_comments.txt file in your directory.
If the file contains text, you have found potential vulnerabilities. The file formats the findings neatly so you can easily review them: [2] 

[URL] https://target.com
[TYPE] JS (External JS File) | [KEYWORDS] key, secret
[CONTENT] const AWS_SECRET = "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
------------------------------------------------------------
[URL] https://target.com
